! DF'S MASK-DEPENDENT HALF -- the fetch does not happen and the whole
! operation is SUPPRESSED.  The companion to df_data_fetch_alarm.strap,
! which runs the mask-ZERO case and scoped this one out because the
! model had no suppression at all.
!
! THE RULE, Reference Manual printed p.32 (PDF 35), verbatim: "When an
! address alarm is caused by a data address used in a fetch-type
! operation, indicator DF is turned on.  If the DF mask bit is one, the
! data fetch is suppressed and the operation terminated.  If the DF
! mask bit is zero, the operation proceeds normally."
!
! AND SAID AGAIN, better, on printed p.45 (PDF 48), indicator 20: "Data
! Fetch (DF).  A computer operation has attempted to fetch data from a
! location in the protected area of storage while the interruption
! system was enabled.  If the corresponding mask bit was one, the fetch
! did not take place, and THE ENTIRE OPERATION WAS SUPPRESSED.  If the
! mask bit was zero, the operation was completed normally."
!
! SUPPRESSED, NOT TERMINATED.  p.32 is the storage-protection chapter
! using "terminated" loosely.  Figure 11, the Indicator List on printed
! p.43, gives DF the class "P,S*,C" with the footnote "*Class S when
! mask is one; otherwise Class C" -- S is suppressed, C is completed --
! and p.51 separates the words outright: "the execution of the
! instruction in progress is either carried through to completion or
! entirely suppressed... When neither is possible, however, execution
! of the instruction is terminated."  p.52-53 give termination three
! ways in and a plain masked-on DF is none of them.
!
! THE ORACLE IS jb_ax5b ROUTINE 1260 TEST 8C, CASE 4, transcribed as
! ax5b_160_test8c.strap ("ENABLE MASK 1 NO FETCH IND ON INTRPT").  Its
! offending fetch is LV,$X3,160AD3 with DF's mask bit stored ONE, and
! its error text if the value arrives anyway is "-FETCHED ABOVE UB WITH
! MASK 1 AND SYSTEM ENABLED."  Case 3 is the same code with the mask at
! ZERO and errors the other way -- "-UNABLE TO FETCH ABOVE UB WITH MASK
! 0, SYSTEM ENABLED." -- and case 2 is enabled's mirror: mask ONE but
! the system DISABLED, where the deck demands the fetch proceed anyway
! ("-UNABLE TO FETCH ABOVE UB WITH MASK 1, SYSTEM DISABLED.").  Those
! three conditions are blocks A, B and C below, in that order.
!
! 8C CANNOT BE RUN HERE (it needs SSIP's 160S1/160SX1 harness and the
! 64-word interrupt table at 160IT), so this is the hand-written stand-
! in for it, written to the shape 8C's own code takes.
!
! WHAT 8C'S PROBE IS, AND WHY IT IS COPIED EXACTLY.  The deck does NOT
! ask whether the result indicators were set.  It preloads X3 with a
! NONZERO value (LVI,$X3,(8)777777.40), zeroes the protected word
! (Z,160AD3), runs the fetch, and then asks for the index VALUE with
! LX,$X3,$X3 + BXVZ / BZXVZ.  A model that "suppressed" by writing zero
! to the destination would pass an indicator check and fail that one.
! So every block below preloads its destination with a value DIFFERENT
! from the protected operand and asks what the destination holds
! afterwards, never what the operation's own indicators say.
!
! p.32'S CLASS 1 ONLY, which is a scope statement.  "Address monitoring
! for Data Fetch includes: 1. The effective address of all fetch-type
! operations with a direct address" -- the four instruction formats
! this model signals at, and the four blocks below.  CLASS 3, the
! refill addresses, sets the indicator but does NOT suppress, and the
! two halves of that gap are different: the progressive-indexing
! refills fall under p.53's "an alert of Class S during progressive
! indexing suppresses only the indexing portion of the operation",
! which is a different (terminating) mechanism; the refills of R, RCZ,
! CBR, V+CR, V+ICR and V-ICR are not progressive indexing at all and
! are a plain unimplemented gap.  dfSuppresses in StretchExecute.bsv
! carries the same statement.
!
! TWO MORE THINGS THIS PROGRAM DOES NOT COVER, said rather than left
! to be found:
!   * A CROSSING FIELD whose SECOND word alone is protected.  The
!     model asks dfSuppresses about both words, but no block here
!     arranges a field that straddles the lower boundary.  The
!     existing class-1 tests do not cover it either.
!   * p.52's read-only BRANCH ON BIT, which is a fifth class-1 site
!     the model has never had at all: "The addressed bit is specified
!     to remain unconditionally unchanged, and the DF mask bit is one.
!     The instruction is suppressed without a test of the addressed
!     bit, and indicator DF is actuated."  Quoted in full on
!     dfSuppresses; not built, and not tested here.
!
! WHAT SUPPRESSION LEAVES BEHIND (p.52): "the suppression of an
! instruction must necessarily exclude the following operations: ...
! 3. The stepping of the instruction counter."  Every block below
! relies on it -- control reaches the check after the suppressed
! instruction, it does not stall or repeat.
!
! THE NOPS AFTER EACH MASK-ONE FETCH ARE NOT PADDING, same reason
! df_data_fetch_alarm.strap and if_branch_alarm.strap give: DF is
! masked ON in block C, so the violation is reported by INTERRUPTING,
! and this model takes an interruption at a QUIESCENT boundary rather
! than at the instruction boundary p.48 names.  The marker is word 7,
! which is in p.31's 0-31 carve-out and so can never itself signal.
!
! EXPECTED NOISE, and a real interaction worth knowing about.  This
! program makes StretchCore print "E-box instruction at 4327 / 4349 /
! 4370 executed at preparation (classification mismatch)", once for
! each of blocks C2, C3 and C4.  It is not a defect and it is not
! spurious -- it is exactly what run_tests.sh already records for
! ad_indicator.strap's AD guard.  The core probes an operand-fetching
! E-box instruction by running execute() with no operand and expecting
! it to bail out with the address it wants; a SUPPRESSED operation
! reads nothing, so the instruction completes during what was meant to
! be a side-effect-free probe.  The core detects exactly that, says so,
! and commits the probe as the execution -- which is why DF is set, the
! accumulator is untouched and the instruction counter still steps
! (p.52 item 3).  The classification cannot know in advance, because
! suppression depends on the effective address and the mask.  Block C1
! is silent because direct index arithmetic is I-box class and never
! reaches that probe.
!
! SEVEN INJECTIONS, EACH OBSERVED (not predicted).  Reproduce any row
! with `python3 line_addresses.py programs/df_fetch_suppress.strap`.
!
!   suppression removed, VFL site        -> 004173.40  BZB, $AE, $ (C2)
!   suppression removed, connective site -> 004206.40  BZB, $AE, $ (C3)
!   suppression removed, dir-index site  -> 004161.00  BZXE, $     (C1)
!   suppression removed, floating point  -> 004220.00  BZB, $AE, $ (C4)
!   the MASK gate dropped from            -> 004122.40  BZXE, $    (B1)
!     dfSuppresses (suppress whenever          -- the mask-ZERO case,
!     protViolates fires)                         which is why block B
!                                                 exists at all
!   the ENABLE gate dropped from          -> 004112.00  BZXE, $    (A)
!     dfSuppresses (protViolates ->            -- the disabled control,
!     installed alone)                            which therefore does
!                                                 discriminate, against
!                                                 expectation
!   dfSignal skipped when suppressing     -> 004170.40  BZB, 7.0, $ (C2)
!     (p.52 item 1 violated)                   -- no indicator, so no
!                                                 interruption, so the
!                                                 routine never ran
!
! Every one halted at its own block, and no injection survived.
!
! Instructions: BD, BE, LVI, SV, LV, KVI, L(BU), K(BU), C0011(BU),
! L(U), K(U), BB, BB1, BBZ, BZB, B, BEW -- all implemented.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
START   BD,  $+.32                   ! disable: words 3 and 12 come next
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA
        LVI, $X13, WLOW
        SV,  $X13, $LB               ! lower boundary := WLOW
        LVI, $X13, WABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB               ! upper boundary := WABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 0: signal INSIDE
!
!   Plant the direct-index operand inside the area while the system is
!   still disabled, so the store itself cannot raise DS.  SV writes the
!   index VALUE into the top 24 bits of the half-word and LV reads it
!   back from there, so the two agree by construction and the program
!   never has to hand-compute a DD's bit layout.
        LVI, $X12, (8)5252
        SV,  $X12, WLOW
!
! ---- A. DISABLED, MASK ONE: the fetch proceeds anyway -------------
!   8C case 2, and it is NOT redundant with case 1.  Disabled, the deck
!   asserts the mask bit is IRRELEVANT: it sets the mask to one and
!   still demands the fetch land.  p.32: "When the interruption system
!   is disabled, address monitoring is ineffective... As a result, the
!   DS, DF and IF indicators cannot be turned on when the system is
!   disabled."
!
!   THIS BLOCK IS CARRIED BY THE PRE-EXISTING ENABLE GATE inside
!   protViolates, which dfSuppresses shares, so it adds no
!   discrimination for the new code.  Recorded here rather than
!   discovered as a surviving injection.
        BB1, 12.20, $+1.0            ! DF's mask bit := ONE
        LVI, $X14, (8)7777           ! a value that is NOT the operand
        BBZ, $XE,  $+1.0             ! clear XE: a check that cannot
                                     !   fail is not a check
        LV,  $X14, WLOW              ! protected word -- but DISABLED
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if DF lit while disabled
        KVI, $X14, (8)5252
        BZXE, $                      ! hang if the operand did NOT arrive
!
! ---- B. ENABLED, MASK ZERO: DF sets and the operation PROCEEDS ----
!   8C case 3.  This is the half df_data_fetch_alarm.strap already
!   tests for the indicator, repeated here at all four sites because
!   IT IS WHAT MAKES THE MASK GATE DISCRIMINATING: without a mask-zero
!   case beside each mask-one case, dropping `maskedOn(st, ind_DF)`
!   from dfSuppresses changes nothing this program can see.
        BBZ, 12.20, $+1.0            ! DF's mask bit := ZERO
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0             ! start from DF off
        BE,  $+.32                   ! ENABLE
!
! ---- B1. direct index arithmetic ---------------------------------
        LVI, $X14, (8)7777
        BBZ, $XE,  $+1.0
        LV,  $X14, WLOW
        BZB, $DF,  $                 ! hang if the fetch did not light DF
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if it lit the NEIGHBOUR: DF is
                                     !   20 and IF is 21, and without this
                                     !   the injection that swaps them is
                                     !   indistinguishable from removal
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
        KVI, $X14, (8)5252
        BZXE, $                      ! hang if the fetch was suppressed
                                     !   with the mask at ZERO
!
! ---- B2. VFL-format fetch (L) ------------------------------------
        L(BU,64,8), PAT2W            ! accumulator := a value that is
                                     !   NOT what WHIGH holds
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        L(BU,64,8), WHIGH            ! protected, mask 0 -> proceeds
        BZB, $DF,  $
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
        K(BU,64,8), PATW             ! PATW holds exactly what WHIGH does
        BZB, $AE,  $                 ! hang if the operand never arrived
!
! ---- B3. a connective whose result reaches the accumulator -------
!   C0011 is f(m,a) = m (Reference Manual Figure 20; applyConnective's
!   own bits are notM&notA / notM&a / m&notA / m&a), so CONNECT copies
!   the storage field into the accumulator field and the accumulator is
!   a direct witness of whether the fetch landed.  CT would not be --
!   its result "is not placed anywhere" (p.76) -- which is why
!   df_data_fetch_alarm.strap's CT line cannot be reused here.
        L(BU,64,8), PAT2W
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        C0011(BU,64), WHIGH          ! protected, mask 0 -> proceeds
        BZB, $DF,  $
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
        K(BU,64,8), PATW
        BZB, $AE,  $
!
! ---- B4. the floating-point operand fetch ------------------------
!   L(U) is the UNNORMALIZED floating-point load, so the accumulator
!   receives the operand unchanged and K(U) against the same bits gives
!   AE -- the pattern bb_indicator.strap already relies on.
        L(U), PAT2W
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        L(U), WHIGH                  ! protected, mask 0 -> proceeds
        BZB, $DF,  $
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
        K(U), PATW
        BZB, $AE,  $
!
! ---- C. ENABLED, MASK ONE: the whole operation is SUPPRESSED ------
!   8C case 4.  The indicator still sets -- p.52 item 1 excludes from
!   suppression "the actuation of those indicators, associated with the
!   instruction, of Class S" -- and then INTERRUPTS, because the mask
!   bit is one.  rl_interrupt turns the causing indicator off on its
!   way into the routine (p.48), so after each block below DF reads
!   OFF and the witness is the routine's own marker, word 7.
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0             ! clear DF before arming its mask
        BB1, 12.20, $+1.0            ! DF's mask bit := ONE
!
! ---- C1. direct index arithmetic ---------------------------------
        LVI, $X14, (8)7777           ! the PRELOAD, which must survive
        BBZ, $XE,  $+1.0
        LV,  $X14, WLOW              ! protected, mask 1 -> SUPPRESSED
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 7.0,  $                 ! hang if DF's routine did not run
        BBZ, 7.0,  $+1.0
        KVI, $X14, (8)7777
        BZXE, $                      ! hang if the fetch landed after all
!
! ---- C2. VFL-format fetch (L) ------------------------------------
        L(BU,64,8), PAT2W            ! the PRELOAD
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        L(BU,64,8), WHIGH            ! protected, mask 1 -> SUPPRESSED
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 7.0,  $
        BBZ, 7.0,  $+1.0
        K(BU,64,8), PAT2W            ! the accumulator must still hold it
        BZB, $AE,  $
!
! ---- C3. the connective ------------------------------------------
        L(BU,64,8), PAT2W
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        C0011(BU,64), WHIGH          ! protected, mask 1 -> SUPPRESSED
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 7.0,  $
        BBZ, 7.0,  $+1.0
        K(BU,64,8), PAT2W
        BZB, $AE,  $
!
! ---- C4. the floating-point operand fetch ------------------------
        L(U), PAT2W
        BBZ, $AE,  $+1.0
        L(U), WHIGH                  ! protected, mask 1 -> SUPPRESSED
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BZB, 7.0,  $
        BBZ, 7.0,  $+1.0
        K(U), PAT2W
        BZB, $AE,  $
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the monitored area and its neighbours -----------------------
!   The area is the HALF-OPEN interval [WLOW, WABOVE) (p.31), so WLOW
!   and WHIGH are protected and WABOVE is the first word outside it.
!   PATW and PAT2W sit BELOW the lower boundary and are never
!   monitored; PATW holds exactly what WHIGH holds, PAT2W something
!   else, and every block reads the accumulator against one of the two.
        SLC, (8)4276.0
PATW    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525252525240
PAT2W   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525212525240
        SLC, (8)4300.0
WLOW    DD(BU,64,8), 0               ! == the lower boundary; the SV
                                     !   above plants the operand here
WHIGH   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525252525240   ! inside the area
WABOVE  DD(BU,64,8), 0               ! == the upper boundary: OUTSIDE
!
! ---- the interruption table --------------------------------------
!   Entry N is at TBL + N words (p.48).  DF is indicator 20, so entry
!   20 is TBL + 0o24.  The DS entry is kept for the same reason
!   if_branch_alarm.strap keeps it: DS is permanently masked on, and a
!   stray store would otherwise run off into unloaded storage.
        SLC, (8)4500.0
TBL     DD, 0
        SLC, (8)4523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS
        SLC, (8)4524.0
DFRTN   BB1, 7.0, $+1.0              ! entry 20 = DF (0o4500 + 0o24)
        END, START
